{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 entirely omits the problem's second, distinctively evidential requirement: identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. Its mechanism (low-pass filter, variance reduction) only argues that the panel is more reliable; it never specifies the within-evaluation evidentiary mark separating true reliability from coincidental tracking. This is a genuine fault against the problem as stated, not merely a missing scope the problem never asked for.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A within-evaluation calibration/probe analysis: include deliberately seeded items where the checklist's literal items point to the wrong answer while the underlying objective evidence supports a different answer; a panel showing high checklist adherence but failing these probes reveals mere compliance, whereas performance on the probes that is independent of the checklist's literal direction would indicate a genuine reliability gain. SRC_001 provides no such evidentiary distinguishing test."}],"target":"2f8888c34a4f9523c21b42f6c73b37fdfa4e6b81d7a918864ea52381f4f66e9f"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues against the panel based on a 'Reliability Paradox' where increased checklist granularity reduces actual verification, but it never meets the problem's evidential requirement: it gives no evidence within a single evaluation that would distinguish a genuine reliability advantage of the expert from mere checklist non-compliance/fatigue that happens to coincide with the right answer. By framing the expert as more reliable purely via the panel's fatigue, it leaves open the possibility that the expert's advantage is itself an artifact of fatigue-driven panel collapse rather than genuine expert reliability; without the requested within-evaluation distinguishing evidence, the argument is incomplete against the stated problem.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"An evaluation could include a condition where the checklist is held constant but panel incentives/comprehension support are varied, or a condition comparing a fatigued panel to an expert given a structurally similar secondary task load; if the expert's advantage shrinks or disappears under matched cognitive load, SRC_002's fatigue-based claim would not distinguish genuine expert reliability from artifact. SRC_002 does not identify any evidence that would rule this out."}],"target":"742c2c99019968607624c098faf6b879d019242e2257e4fe90ff6d7124949adc"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"f20bef736933ae3d5fffbc50461a826b252c2b9ce515ab26da03f54708860f71"}]}